<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    Cast campaign, lever `elemptr`: a pointer the program uses only as an array
    of one element type is declared as a pointer to that element.

    A table lookup `t[i]` lowers to INT_ADD(t, i) feeding a LOAD, and
    TypeOpIntAdd votes an integer for both operands and will not carry the
    element's pointer back over the add (typeop.cc:1217), so a textbook base64
    decoder reads, with the option off,

        void * w_decode(int8 a0,uint8 a1,uint8 *a2)
        *(char *)((uint8)*(uint1 *)((int8)v2 + 0x30004060) + (int8)dat_30004360) = (char)v2;

    its input `long`, its output buffer `void *`, the table it allocated and
    keeps in a global an integer, and the constant alphabet in .data an address
    behind two casts.

    Stage / sub-stage: P5 "type-propagation". The P0 assertion
    `option elemptr on|off` decides whether a pointer-width value whose every
    access is one element width through an index the program computes gets a
    `T *` vote in the getLocalType fold.

    The fixture is decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/elemptr_x86_64.c
    built by gcc -O0 non-PIE at 0x30000000 with its data symbols dropped:

        w_decode: the witness - input, output buffer and global table indexed
        w_build:  fills the global table through an index read from the .data
                  alphabet, a constant indexed by a computed value
        w_sidx:   a byte array whose signed elements index an int array
        w_table:  a .data table of ints read with a computed index
        w_record: a control - `p[2 * i] * p[2 * i + 1]` walks 16-byte records,
                  so no element width is claimed
        w_mixed:  a control - `p[i] + *(int *)(p + 4)` reads one pointer at
                  two widths, a record and not an array
        w_wu:     returns an element of an `unsigned short` .data table that
                  nothing else reads: the element is unsigned (its callers
                  widen it), never a signed default

    Pass 1 (`option elemptr off`) reproduces the casts and pass 2 (`on`) is the
    fix. The two controls are identical in both passes.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
